.TH WB\-STATUS 1 "" "watchbill"
.SH NAME
wb\-status \- list the issues in the flow and where each stands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwb\fR status [<issue>] [\-\-stage <heading>] [\-q | \-\-quiet]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
List the issues in the flow under headings, as git status lists files: each issue under the last step it has done (Not planned, Planned, Accepted, Tested, Built, Reviewed, Fixed), and last the issues that have a question no [answers] or [answer] line has closed. An issue is in the flow when its branch, local or at origin, holds a commit that the default branch of origin does not: git alone says which, and fetches nothing. Its line shows the branch, the open change as #<n> (change_of_branch) and how many commits are not pushed.
.PP
The stamps are the commits on the job's ref that count. The questions and answers of a job whose ref holds a questions commit are read from the ref, and no issue_comments is run for it; those of any other job come from issue_comments. For an open question read from the ref it says on standard error that an answer typed at the tracker is not read. An acceptance that a source or a planner round after it voided puts the issue under Planned. An acceptance counts only where its signed tag checks against the keys of .watchbill/signers of the default branch with the role accept, and a stamp only where it is signed by a key there with start; <issue> says under Worked out from by whom it is signed, or why it counts for nothing (the tag not there, unsigned, a key not in the list, a key without accept, a signature that does not check, another job, another repository: a base that is no commit here, or a job that is not the ref's). Where the default branch holds no list, or one with no key line, or there is none, nothing that needs a signature counts, and <issue> says once: no .watchbill/signers on <branch>: nothing is signed, so nothing counts. Where the job's ref is not as wb writes it (one line of commits, with no merge, each signed by a key of the list with the role it needs, and each stamp's tree its parent's but for view and issue.yml), nothing on it counts, the acceptance counts as void, and <issue> names the first commit that is not, on the line of first parents, on one line. <issue> prints that issue, in the flow or not, with its title (issue_address) and what it was worked out from; \-\-stage <heading> prints that heading only, whatever its case. A job whose work is accepted, or whose change is merged, is done: it is not listed, and <issue> prints Done and what closed it, and a line accepted: for each acceptance, by its object. For one issue it also prints a line kept: <role> round <n>, <k> commits not taken, <folder> for each view kept with work not taken back, and cut off: <role> round <n>, <folder> for each session whose wb ended before it did, and running: <role> round <n>, <run time>, quiet <quiet time> for each session that runs, its wb alive, as 'running: build round 2, 7h12m, quiet 6h50m'. With no issue it says on standard error how many such views are kept on this machine. A stamp of the tester, the build or the fix puts an issue at its step only where a commit of its round came back, as the lines of the starts on this machine say; without them the stamp counts. For one issue it prints <role> round <n>: no commit came back for each round that brought nothing back, and goes on with kept: <what it holds>, <folder> where a view is kept for it. A view of over 200 commits is said as the first 200 and 'and <k> more', and each view is read within 20 seconds. Ends with 0 when every issue could be read, 1 when a pattern failed for one (it is listed under Not read), 2 when it was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.PP
Writes nothing but a temporary repository in $TMPDIR, removed after, where it reads a kept view.
.PP
\-q (\-\-quiet) is taken and changes nothing: what it prints is what a script reads.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B <issue>
Print that issue, in the flow or not, with its title (issue_address) and what it was worked out from.
.TP
.B \-\-stage <heading>
Print that heading only, whatever its case.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Taken, and changes nothing: what the command prints is what a script reads.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every issue could be read.
.TP
.B 1
A pattern failed for one issue. It is listed under Not read.
.TP
.B 2
It was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wb (1) ,
.BR wb\-answer (1) ,
.BR wb\-start (1)
